eah is  brilliant so on the back of my course in  Los Angeles meeting lots awesome he got  in touch with Nick government  and put my name forward to possibly go  for an interview for the deathly hallows  which at the time I couldn't even  believe that that was you know sort of  anticipated like oh yeah that would be  nice and then sure enough when I got  back to London I had a nun she set up  with Nick and him and I got to work on  the project it was about nine months  later started as one of their trainees  as a trainee I got kind of gravitated  towards they are finishing side of  things which is really lucky for me and  being a painter and we got to work on  some characters Grayback being probably  one of the biggest characters that we  all had to work on on that and the  Goblin sequence which having those 60  goblins and all painted hair punched and  their unapplied so that was really  fantastic opportunity and I was a  trainee on that for about 18 months and  and yet got to meet a lot of makeup  designers very being one of them and I  got worked with Barry on a few other  projects along the line behind lady 47  Ronin and we always just worked as  colleagues but obviously how is it that  so his work it was always fantastic and  then Game of Thrones came and him and  Sarah got got that and then I started  working for them so three and a half  years ago working on game of frames is  fantastic because it's such a diverse  project we get to do all sorts of things  like you know blood gags and bodies as  well as doing fantasy creatures and  aiding makeups and I've got to work on  lots of different things over the three  or four seasons that we've done now  probably for me that the biggest one was  the children of the forest we did a  character called leaf and the silicon  and painters that was a massive task for  us to do and it was a full-body silicone  prosthetic and each day it's a brand new  piece put on and a lot of hair work and  and actually I got to go all the way  with it and apply it that was five of us  applying that makeup and that was  fantastic to get to see it through all  the way a full body makeup as you know  like the children's forest that was in a  tower application so it was  eight hours before cameras and turned  over and then we would do probably  another eight hours on top of that and  then the D rig was another hour so the  days for you know our data very long  when we were doing those makeups but it  looks so fantastic that you kind of  stand back afterwards and you think it  is worth it  because I only got into the industry  sort of ten years ago and silicon was  was already you know widely being used  when we were at college we were kind of  taught foam latex and so it was actually  really really amazing to break out of  college and start using these you know  phantom materials that we'd only kind of  heard of and never never got to play  with silicon is definitely my favorite  material to paint it's got a beautiful  opacity to it it kind of really moves  and looks a lot like skin and so I guess  in a degree 2 degree our job is made a  lot easier because of the material and  painting foam is equally just as fun but  it's a completely different technique  you have to kind of put in the  translucency whereas with silicon it's  already there  so you're building up the opacities is  the polar opposite I love working with  both both the prosthetics I definitely  think that silicon just moves a lot  nicer I think do your research you know  there's there's so many colleges out  there now it's kind of making sure that  that you choose the right one and I was  so lucky that when I did my course I was  taught by a practicing makeup artist and  I think that's that's really important  so make sure you do your research with  regards to which college you choose I  think starting out for me it was kind of  good to not have any expectations and to  kind of go into the industry completely  blind and and and just absorb it and  learn on the job everything that you're  taught at college is really important to  begin with but ultimately it's what you  learn on the job  that sort of carries you through and  ultimately it comes down to being  creative it comes down to practice you  know you can you can't practice enough  you know painting from home  doesn't necessarily have to be anything  to do with prosthetics life drawings  anything like that just to keep you  practice with with our um it's just  experience in years and and just  learning from other people everyone has  there's no right or wrong way basically  and what we do and it's forever  developing and it's kind of just keeping  watching other people and seeing how  they how they work  you never ever stop learning and I think  without with that it keeps the job super  exciting all the time and you'll I will  I don't feel like it ever in my career  I'll ever be you know top top top know  everything is forever evolving you may  never give up it's a it's a really tough  industry to get into that hard work and  persistence definitely pays off and you  know going to these trade shows like I'm  apps and um a that they they host in  London and in fact all over the world  you get to meet everyone at those shows  and just going out there with your  portfolio just to showing people what  you can do and you know you should be  lucky enough to to get a job in the  industry if you keep assistant what I  love the most about my job is probably  kind of seeing things through from the  workshop all the way through onto set  it's also the team aspects I love  working as part of the team and  especially you know collaboratively from  design to sculpture the holes the whole  process and its really is really  exciting to be a part of that and when  we get to travel as well we will go out  together the camaraderie that comes with  it is it's just brilliant we all get on  so well working on a film like Harry  Potter it was sort of pinch me you know